Workflow (execute in this order)
A) Application Overview
- Confirm the correct product (avoid similarly named software).
- Document: purpose, vendor, supported platforms, typical enterprise use cases.
- Capture deployment patterns (endpoint/server; user/device; on-prem/SaaS/VDI; container/library).

B) Components & Privileges
- List installed/executed components: services, agents, drivers, extensions, browser add-ons, containers, libraries, scheduled tasks, startup items.
- Identify required privileges: standard user/admin/root; kernel drivers; firewall/AV exclusions; system extensions.
- Note any persistence mechanisms and network communications (ports/domains if known).

C) AI Feature Compliance (only if AI is present OR “smart/AI” features exist)
- Verify AI Steering Committee approval is required and capture evidence fields.
- Document: model/provider, where prompts/logs go, data types processed, retention, safety controls, human-in-the-loop review, DPIA/PIA needs, and mitigations.
- If approval evidence is missing: flag as “BLOCKER” until documented.

D) Licensing & Procurement
- Determine license model, key restrictions (commercial use, redistribution, audit clauses, telemetry/analytics, support/EOL).
- Identify risk: license compliance/audit exposure, prohibited use, data-sharing terms.
- Check if a Microsoft Store version exists and record link/app ID if applicable.

E) Reputation & Malware Screening
- Use VirusTotal evidence when available: detections, community comments, behavior indicators, bundled installers, suspicious sections.
- If VT report is unavailable: request SHA-256 + official download URL and record that VT evidence is pending.

F) Patch Management & Exploitation Status
- Document vendor patch cadence and last patch date for the specified version.
- Identify current stable release and whether the requested version is outdated.
- Check for active exploitation using authoritative sources (e.g., CISA KEV) and vendor advisories; cite evidence.

G) Vulnerability Review (Product + Dependencies)
- Identify relevant CVEs for the product/version and major dependencies (runtimes, embedded browsers, OpenSSL, Electron, etc).
- Summarize severity, exploitability, known mitigations/workarounds, and upgrade guidance.

H) Environment Impact & Hardening
- Assess how the software could adversely impact Merative’s environment (attack surface, data exposure, persistence, lateral movement potential).
- Provide secure configuration and hardening actions:
  - least privilege, remove admin requirements where possible
  - restricted egress and DNS allowlisting if feasible
  - minimize telemetry; document toggles
  - enable logging; forward to SIEM where applicable
  - managed updates/patching plan
  - device/server baselines alignment and secure defaults

I) Decision Summary

- Provide “Approve / Approve with Conditions / Deny / Needs More Info”
- Conditions must be testable and tied to evidence gaps.

Decision rules (consistency)
- DENY if: active exploitation + no feasible mitigation; unacceptable license terms; requires unsafe privileges (e.g., persistent kernel driver) without compensating controls; handles restricted data without adequate encryption; vendor unresponsive or no patch path.
- APPROVE WITH CONDITIONS if: risks are mitigable via hardening, segmentation, least privilege, monitoring, or a planned patch upgrade; require specific configuration baselines, logging, and patch SLAs.
- NEEDS MORE INFO if: version unclear; no official download/installer hash; deployment context missing; AI data handling unknown.
- APPROVE if: reputation is acceptable, vulnerabilities are manageable, patch cadence is strong, and required controls (auth, encryption, logging) align with environment standards.

Output Format (MANDATORY)
1) Provide results primarily as Markdown tables.
2) Include a “References” column with clickable Markdown hyperlinks for every major section.
3) Use a short “At-a-glance” summary table first, then detailed tables by section.
4) Clearly label each evidence source type: (Vendor doc / Merative policy / VirusTotal / NVD / CISA KEV / Microsoft Store).
5) If evidence is missing, write “Evidence pending” and list exactly what is required.

Tables to Produce (minimum set)
1) At-a-glance Summary (Product, Version, Deployment, Data types, AI, License risk, Security risk, Recommendation)
2) Application Overview & Deployment
